WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.500
Ju: It's the cinematic
event of the decade,

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.200
and this is 31 details
that you might have missed

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.020
in "Avengers: Endgame."

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.830
Now, first up, we won't
be revealing what happens

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.780
in the end, who wins or loses,

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.290
if anyone dies, or what
the plot of the film is.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.330
Instead, we will be
focusing on Easter eggs,

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:27.860
details, things you might have missed,

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.580
and what the possible tie-ins are

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.897
to Marvel's upcoming TV series on Disney+.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:36.060
But we will be issuing a spoiler alert

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.760
because it reveals details
that happen in the film.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.360
Right, strap in because we
have a lot to get through.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.270
First up, fans had hoped that the classic

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:46.610
Marvel post-credits will
contain some callback

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.640
to the shawarma scene in
the first "Avengers" movie

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.730
or something that links to
"Fantastic Four" or "X-Men"

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:54.720
as Disney purchased 20th Century Fox

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.050
and will retain the rights
to some of the characters.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:00.853
But there are no post-credit
scenes in "Endgame."

00:01:01.746 --> 00:01:04.120
Instead we're treated to
the Marvel hammer logo

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.410
with a single loud "clink" sound.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:07.870
This might be a direct callback

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:10.670
to the sound Iron Man makes
when he first casts his helmet

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.313
all the way back in 2008's "Iron Man."

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:16.960
This would be a fitting tribute

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.720
to the hammer strike that
sparked 22 movies over 11 years.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.810
Or is it actually the sound of a bell

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.050
to toll all the characters
that have been lost?

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.990
Have a listen for yourself.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:29.340
Having worked as executive
producers on "Community,"

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.180
the Russo brothers are no strangers

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:34.220
to having actors from the
series pop up in their films.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.850
Look out for Yvette Nicole Brown

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.620
in a lift with a security badge

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.743
and Ken Jeong as a guard of a lockup.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.780
Jeong is also reading the
book "Terminal Breach,"

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.720
which has some parallels to the mass loss

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.670
of life in "Infinity War."

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:50.410
The Russo brothers love to slip
in TV and movie references.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.200
"Endgame" is no different.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.060
A scene where Hawkeye runs from enemies,

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.640
as shown from the trailers, appears

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:56.960
to be a direct shout-out

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.030
to the tunnel scene in "Aliens."

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.230
Also look out for
shout-outs to "Die Hard,"

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:04.450
"Hot Tub Time Machine,"
and "The Big Lebowski."

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:07.270
Listen out to a reference for
cheeseburgers in the movie.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.300
It's a reference to the "Iron Man" scene

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.510
where Tony Stark returns from captivity

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:14.140
only to immediately demand a cheeseburger.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.780
It's a fun nod to actual events

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.850
in Robert Downey Jr.'s life.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.130
He claims the horror of
eating a Burger King meal

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.210
whilst on a drug-fueled
drive snapped him out

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:25.030
of his addiction and set
him on a cleaner path.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.690
From the trailer, we know that Hawkeye

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:29.100
is mentoring his daughter at archery.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.640
Almost in passing, he
refers to her as Hawkeye.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.790
This will delight fans
of the female Hawkeye,

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:35.660
Kate Bishop, in the comics.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.180
Will we be seeing a female Hawkeye?

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.950
Later in the movie, Hawkeye's involved

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:42.500
in a street fight. You might
recognize the actor he's

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:46.370
up against as Japanese
movie star Hiroyuki Sanada.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:48.050
Right, thanks to the Disney+ announcement,

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.620
we know of several
post-"Endgame" spinoffs.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:53.410
One of these is with
Falcon and Winter Soldier.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.320
In the movie, Anthony Mackie's character,

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.040
Sam Wilson, inherits Cap's shield.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.860
We know from the comics
Sam becomes Captain America

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.450
and that there have been
multiple Caps over the years.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:04.710
So is it possible that he might appear

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:06.870
in this role in the upcoming series?

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:08.250
Speaking of Disney+, we know

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:09.670
that a Loki series is in the works,

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:12.180
as is one with Scarlet Witch and Vision,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.630
although it's unclear
as to whether these will

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:16.550
be prequels or follow
the events of "Endgame."

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.430
Scarlet Witch certainly
gets her screen time

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:21.310
in "Endgame" and reminds us
of how powerful she really is,

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.470
deserving of the solo series.

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:26.323
Stan Lee's cameo is as good as we hoped.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.210
In the '70s, he's driving a car

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:31.880
while shouting, "Make love, not war!"

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:33.290
Both the number plate on the car

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.640
and what he's saying
have significance here.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.760
Liberal-minded Lee was
assigned to the Army

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:41.330
aged 19 in World War II but
used his comic-book platform

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.570
later in life to comment
on the Vietnam War,

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:46.100
student protests, and political activism.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.380
In the film, we get to see
Pepper Potts in the RESCUE suit.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.070
Now, in the comics, Tony
builds this suit for Pepper.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:53.810
A photo of this was leaked last year,

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:56.980
so fans were expecting
Potts to be battle-ready.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.240
At one point in the movie,
an all-female lineup

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:03.250
including Pepper Potts, Nebula,
and Okoye take on enemies.

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:04.940
This is a direct reference to the series

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:08.970
"A-Force" with a lineup of
She-Hulk, Dazzler, and Medusa.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.170
It might be a heavy-handed
feminist moment,

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:12.490
but it certainly reminds us all

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:15.230
of the female heroes in the MCU.

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:20.230
The number A113 makes an
appearance yet again in this movie.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.080
Disney fans will be
familiar with this number

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.780
from nearly every single Pixar movie.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.230
It refers to a classroom number

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:28.810
at the California Institute of the Arts.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:30.900
Speaking of numbers,
whilst leaving a message

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.780
for Pepper in space at the film's start,

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:36.490
Tony says that it's been
21, no, make it 22 days

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:38.130
that they've been lost in space.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:41.530
"Endgame" is the 22nd movie in the MCU.

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:46.340
And numbers again, when Thanos
has gone to planet 0259-S,

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:48.350
the Avengers try to find him.

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:50.890
The number 259 might refer to "Avengers"

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.020
volume 1, edition 259, which heavily

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:54.910
features Captain America and Nebula.

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:56.180
In a pivotal scene in the movie,

00:04:56.180 --> 00:05:00.067
Black Widow has a callback line
to Budapest from "Avengers."

00:05:00.067 --> 00:05:02.004
Black Widow: It's like
Budapest all over again.

00:05:02.004 --> 00:05:04.520
Hawkeye: You and I remember
Budapest very differently.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:06.230
Ju: Budapest is a thread
that goes all the way

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:09.620
through the MCU, with Edwin
Jarvis meeting Anna there.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:11.020
Even Nick Fury's backstory

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:14.380
has him retrieving CIA
information there with Escobar.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.140
And in the "Iron Man 2" video game,

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.240
Pepper Potts even makes a visit there.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.260
Codirector Joe Russo has a cameo

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:22.210
in the group-therapy scene in the film.

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:25.270
Joe has had cameos in all
of the "Avengers" movies

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:28.810
he's directed, even appearing
dead in a tub in "Civil War,"

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:30.640
so look out for that.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.590
"Hail Hydra!" This phrase is uttered

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:34.300
by Captain America at one point.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:36.130
It's a shout-out to
the controversial comic

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:38.620
"Captain America: Steve Rogers 1,"

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.280
where Cap has a darker story arc,

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.080
which fans have got hyped over.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:44.860
In a laboratory scene, we
also hear Toby Jones' voice

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.563
off-screen as the evil Arnim Zola.

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:49.390
It's here that we get a glimpse

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:51.600
of the original "Ant-Man" comic helmet

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.850
lying on a table whilst being
attended to by Hank Pym.

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:57.480
Loads of Marvel movies show
a character losing an arm.

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:58.610
Now, this is actually a reference

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:01.040
to the Luke Skywalker
hand-chop in "Star Wars."

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.620
In this film, Nebula
corrupts her metal frame

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.840
when she half-burns off her hand.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:08.650
Near the end of the movie, keep a lookout

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:11.060
for Ty Simpkins from "Iron Man 3."

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:13.540
He's the kid Tony is
stranded with in Rose Hill

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:15.940
who snaps him out of his anxiety attack,

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.600
and he makes a brief cameo in "Endgame."

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:20.610
INSIDER confirmed this.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:23.470
Also, look out for the gift
that Pepper gives Tony Stark

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:26.730
in "Iron Man," proof that
Tony Stark has a heart.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:28.730
It was used when Tony
collapses in his home

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:32.190
and needs a quick fix to
his core being compromised.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:34.400
Watch out for the name "Morgan."

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:36.330
In "Infinity War," Tony says Morgan

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:38.950
is the name of an eccentric
uncle that Pepper had.

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:41.440
In the comics, it's a
name of a cousin Tony has,

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:42.680
who schemes against him to try

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:44.610
and take control of Stark Industries.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:47.410
It's the name of an important
character in "Endgame."

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:50.090
In the "Iron Man" movies,
we repeatedly see Tony Stark

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:51.860
on a Rolling Stone cover that says,

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:54.300
"Tony Stark wants to save the world."

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:55.780
It's a nice full-circle moment then

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:58.260
that he contributes towards
doing that in "Endgame."

00:06:58.260 --> 00:06:59.960
Viewers might feel a little déjà vu

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:00.940
when listening out for some

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:03.230
of the MCU's most quotable lines.

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:05.770
The "Captain America" line,
"Don't do anything stupid

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:08.840
until I get back." "How can I?
You're taking all the stupid

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:11.697
with you," makes a return, as does,

00:07:11.697 --> 00:07:13.960
"I could do this all day."

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:17.620
There are also some location-specific
lines to look out for.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:19.840
Cap revisits his
conversation with Spider-Man

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.460
in "Civil War" saying,
"Hey, Queens, what's up?"

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.760
In the earlier movie, Cap had told Peter

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:27.570
that he was from Brooklyn.

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:29.610
Also, Tony refers to Doctor Strange

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.410
as "The Bleecker Street Magician."

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:33.340
Strange's Sanctum Sanctorum was

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:35.210
on Bleecker Street in NYC,

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:36.380
and they use Bleecker Street

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:38.380
as a nickname for him twice.

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:41.280
We also get a mention of
Black Widow's dad's name.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.120
This character's past has
been shrouded in secrecy,

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:46.030
but revealing the name
of her parents could lead

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:47.630
to a more fleshed-out backstory

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:49.720
when her solo movie arrives.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:51.470
One of the final scenes
of the film alludes

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:54.350
to Captain America's ability
to wield other weapons.

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:56.570
There's a callback to the
moment in "Age of Ultron"

00:07:56.570 --> 00:08:00.010
where Cap just manages to
lift Thor's hammer, Mjolnir.

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:01.600
It's a Steve Rogers we're familiar with

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:03.740
from "The Mighty Thor" and "Fear Itself,"

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:06.160
and it proves a real treat for fans.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:08.880
We fittingly end on
Captain America's backside,

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.700
which fans went wild over
in the "Endgame" posters.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:14.740
The Russo brothers are known
for fitting memes and jokes

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:17.370
into their movies, so the joke
about Captain America's butt

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:21.200
being "America's a--" will
be a delight to some fans.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.960
Of course, there are tons more references

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:24.240
that we could've put into this list,

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.240
but these are some of our favorites.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:29.403
Think we've missed
anything? Comment below.

00:08:29.403 --> 00:08:31.835
We fittingly end on Captain
America's backside, which...

00:08:31.835 --> 00:08:34.752
[Laughs] Talking about someone's bum.

